</div>

<h2>Pricing deep-dive by model</h2>
<h3>Usage-based messaging</h3>
<p>
You pay per segment sent or delivered, usually plus a carrier pass-through and a platform floor. The model scales down gracefully and matches cost to activity, which is why most SMS-native tools use it. The risk is campaign success: a BFCM broadcast to a large list can produce a one-month bill larger than the previous quarter. Ask each vendor to quote your actual mix — average segments per message, MMS share, monthly volume, and peak-month volume — and confirm whether undelivered messages still bill.
</p>
<h3>Contact- or profile-based</h3>
<p>
You pay for stored contacts or active profiles, with messaging bundled or metered. The bill is predictable, which finance teams like, but dormant records cost the same as engaged ones until someone prunes. Profile hygiene becomes a paid discipline: dedupe identities, sunset non-engagers, and watch how the platform counts a "profile" across email and SMS. Model the bill at 2× current list size, not current size.
</p>
<h3>Custom enterprise contracts</h3>
<p>
Platform minimums, negotiated message rates, managed services, and term commitments. This model buys expertise and volume economics, and it also raises the cost of a wrong fit: onboarding fees, multi-month minimums, and notice periods make an exit expensive. Ask for carrier fees, onboarding costs, support scope, and cancellation terms in writing, and treat any case-study revenue claim as directional rather than guaranteed.
</p>

<h2>Negotiation checklist: questions that change the invoice</h2>
<ul>
<li>Do undelivered or attempted messages bill, or only delivered ones?</li>
<li>What are the per-message and MMS rates at my peak-month volume, including carrier surcharges?</li>
<li>What happens when I cross a tier mid-campaign — prorated or full next tier?</li>
<li>How are profiles counted across email and SMS, and what cleans a dormant profile?</li>
<li>Which integrations, seats, and reporting views cost extra?</li>
<li>What are onboarding, migration, and data-export fees — in and out?</li>
<li>What is the cancellation notice period, and is there a term minimum?</li>
</ul>
<p>
Get every answer in writing and file it with the quote. When two finalists are close, run the same four-flow pilot in both and let the measured contribution margin — not the sales deck — settle it.
</p>

<h2>Seasonality and the twelve-month view</h2>
<p>
SMS programs are seasonal, and pricing rarely is. A store that sends 20% of its annual messages in November pays either a usage-based bill that spikes with the success it wanted, or a contact-based plan sized for a peak it reaches one month a year. Neither is wrong — but model the year, not the month. Pull your last twelve months of sends and orders, apply each vendor's model at that shape, and include the peak month explicitly rather than averaging it away.
</p>
<p>
Seasonality also changes discount pressure. Peak-season campaigns often need deeper offers to convert, which is exactly when per-message volume is highest; the combination is where margin quietly exits. A flow that earns $40 of contribution in March can earn $12 in November if the discount deepens and MMS share rises. Budget peak-season margin separately, and set a frequency ceiling for peak weeks before the calendar forces one.
</p>
<p>
Finally, revisit the pricing model annually. A store that outgrew usage-based pricing at 10,000 messages a month may find contract terms cheaper at 50,000, and a store that slimmed its list may be overpaying for profile capacity it no longer needs. The right model is a function of your current send shape, not a one-time decision.
</p>

<h2>Matchup FAQ: tools merchants price against each other</h2>
<p>
These are the pairings merchants ask about most, with the pricing lens that actually distinguishes them. The same logic applies to any pairing: identify the billing model, the channel mix, and the discount depth each requires, then let your pilot data decide.
</p>
<h3>Postscript vs. Omnisend on price</h3>
<p>
Postscript bills usage for a specialist SMS operation; Omnisend bundles email, SMS, and push into contact-plus-usage tiers. Postscript usually wins when SMS is the primary job and volume is concentrated; Omnisend usually wins when one small team wants all three channels in one builder. Compare total program cost at your volume, including the discount depth each requires to convert — not the platform fee alone.
</p>
<h3>Klaviyo vs. Sequenzy on price</h3>
<p>
Klaviyo prices by active profiles and SMS usage and rewards teams that will maintain segments and events. Sequenzy is email-first with pay-per-email positioning and no contact-based billing, designed to plan lifecycle email while a dedicated SMS provider handles consented texts. They overlap less than a feature grid suggests: if SMS depth is the requirement, shortlist a specialist; if lifecycle email planning is the requirement, price both and pilot them. Check each vendor's official pricing page for current terms before forecasting.
</p>
<h3>Attentive vs. Postscript at scale</h3>
<p>
Attentive's custom contract buys managed acquisition and service depth; Postscript's usage model stays self-serve. The crossover is program scale and internal headcount: a lean team sending high volume may find managed services cheaper than hiring, while a hands-on retention team may find a contract's minimums restrictive. Request written terms from both and compare total cost including the operator hours each model implies.
</p>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Shopify SMS pricing FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
